<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Buonarroti returns three months after the left thumb-base injection. The planned review four to six weeks after the injection did not take place; a nurse called him at five weeks and recorded that his hand was much better, but no pain or function score was taken. He completed four sessions of hand therapy between mid-May and mid-June and does his thumb exercises each morning. He now carves with padded chisel grips, a lighter mallet, and the splint, and rests his hand every 45 minutes. The finger cut from early June healed, and the sutures came out at 11 days. His pain is about 3 of 10, and he can write a short letter with a thick-barrelled pen. He takes acetaminophen 1 g most mornings and uses the gel twice a day. No gout flare, stones, falls, or ibuprofen. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pain</td><td>About 3 of 10; worse after long sessions</td></tr><tr><td>Function</td><td>Carving about three hours a morning; writes short letters with a thick pen</td></tr><tr><td>Hand therapy</td><td>Four sessions completed; daily home exercises</td></tr><tr><td>Missed review</td><td>No scored review at four to six weeks after the injection; nurse telephone call at five weeks only</td></tr><tr><td>Finger wound</td><td>Healed; sutures out at 11 days</td></tr><tr><td>Analgesics</td><td>Acetaminophen 1 g most mornings; gel twice a day; no oral NSAIDs</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

